# CHAOS Project Rules and Patterns

## Project Structure
- Python libraries are organized in the `libs/` directory
- Each library has its own pyproject.toml and README.md
- Notebooks are stored in the `notebooks/` directory
- Data files are stored in the `data/` directory
- Generated outputs go to the `outputs/` directory

## Coding Patterns
- Python modules follow a clear separation of concerns
- Core algorithms are implemented in dedicated modules
- Utility functions are separated from core algorithms
- Tests follow the naming convention `test_*.py`

## Documentation Patterns
- Memory bank is maintained in the `memory-bank/` directory
- Core documentation files follow the structure defined in the memory bank
- Code should include docstrings for functions and classes
- Notebooks serve as executable documentation for algorithms

## Development Workflow
- Python version is managed via uv (.python-version files)
- Dependencies are defined in pyproject.toml
- Linting is performed using ruff (ruff.toml)
- Unit tests should be written for new functionality

## Project Intelligence
- The project focuses on complex systems analysis and chaos theory
- Key algorithms include Empirical Dynamic Modeling techniques
- The codebase is organized into modular libraries with clear interfaces
- Notebooks demonstrate various applications and techniques

## Learning Journal
- Initial memory bank setup completed on 2025-03-06
- Project appears to be research-oriented with a focus on nonlinear dynamics
- Core libraries (edmkit, generate) implement fundamental algorithms
- Further exploration needed to understand implementation details

## User Preferences
- Maintain clear separation between libraries
- Follow existing code organization patterns
- Document algorithms and their theoretical background
- Ensure tests cover core functionality